e  zone in a time zone ID we reference  America Chicago if you want to look at  sort of the messy history of time zones  there at the United States you can see  that Indiana has had a particular  particularly fractious relationship with  time zones Evansville has been in  central time since the time zone was  incepted but knox county has moved time  zones they've changed offsets regularly  the same thing has happened throughout  part of North Dakota and part of  Wisconsin in these times our IDs take  into account the fact that if I move 60  years back in the past I may have a  different offset than I do  right now we also for convenience allow  low K ssin and it is to be specified for  time zones and will effectively look up  what that times at the time zone  associated with that location is so I  can say my time zone is Bismarck and it  will look up and know okay  Bismarck is in Central time we also  added support for time zone none  recently which allows you to generate a  data object with no explicit time zone  so for example if I have if I generate a  time zone and send it to someone at a  different time zone it will still  represent the same reference point for  them a time zone of none will  effectively delay the use of  instantiation for that time we still  have a reference time that's generated  for the representation but we don't have  an interpretation associated with when  that falls on a GMT offset unless I need  to do something like date list or date  string at which point it will know what  time zone it should use there one notion  that I wanted to mention briefly that  we're working on that isn't immediately  available is time zone notation that  includes a time zone ID and an offset  this is particularly important because  neither of those is completely  sufficient to fully resolve all moments  in time in a couple of weeks we're gonna  transition off of daylight saving time  here and we're gonna have two different  two a.m. so 2 a.m. to 3 a.m. and then we  will reset fallback in our new 2 a.m. to  3 a.m. again if you strictly use a time  zone ID only we don't know which of  those two to a.m. you're talking about  and again in spring when we jump forward  an hour there's an hour that doesn't  exist if we use just the ID or just the  offset we can't fully resolve down that  one period of ambiguity around daylight  savings time and so in the future we  want to have time zones be able to  support both of those specifications so  for that period I could  concretely nail down what 2 a.m. i'm  talking about or if i'm working with  something like a time object that  doesn't have a reference date i'll be  able to say okay this was 6:00 p.m.  central time but it was back  January so it had a different offset  than I have right now so we can see in  these tables that the difference between  the gmt offset and the chicago times  start to diverge after a two hour  difference from the onset or offset of  GMT so the last feature I want to talk  about is one that is not currently in  the Wolfram language but it's something  we're looking at extending which is the  notion of time systems I mentioned at  the beginning that a time system is  somewhat analogous to a datum within a  Geo position it basically tells you how  you measured what time it currently is  if someone asks you what time it is and  you look at your watch you're generally  looking at a watch that's been  synchronized with some other source  typically it's based off of coordinated  Universal Time or UTC which is a  approximation of UT of universal time  standard one and the the complication  here is that throughout history we  basically would look up at the Sun and  use that as our apparent solar time as  our reference point for what time  something was the closer we were at at  noon the higher the Sun was and we had a  good reference point but early on in the  20th century we realized oh the Earth's  orbit isn't perfectly elliptical so  there are different periods where we  have a different offset and you know for  example this graphic at the top shows  that a full rotation of the earth when  we're looking at the Sun we've actually  over corrected for our apparent location  relative to the Sun and if we were to  look at a distant point in space or use  something like a femoris time we would  have a different point on the clock and  the difference of a few microseconds is  not major for many date calculations but  once we start talking about bodies  moving through space the DIF being off  by a second or off by a few milliseconds  can represent a very large change in  distance so we want to be able to  support things like International atomic  time or coordinated Universal Time or  geocentric coordinate time the  coordinate time that's used by GPS  systems which do not account for leap  seconds they don't care about the  rotation of the earth they were  synchronized with Ti a back in the 1960s  and since then don't need to do any  correction because they're not concerned  with exactly how  the earth is rotating and because the  earth is slowly slowing down it means  that we have to keep adding leap seconds  in and there are different ways we  accommodate that because even though  we've redefined the fundamental  definition of what a second is in terms  of cycles of a cesium clock it turns out  that if you put atomic clocks on  different parts of the earth they run at  slightly different speeds because the  geomagnetic forces and the gravity well  of you know of the planet Earth is not  uniform so the other systems like GPS t  simulates an atomic clock that follows  the rotation of the earth but exists  somewhere out in the vacuum of space and  isn't affected by the Earth's  geomagnetic field it's something that  we're looking at implementing in the  Wolfram language and we hope to be able  to do things like map between these  different coordinate systems of time so  that we can handle astronomical  calculations and things in